<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maximilian I of Mexico</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1832">1832</a>–<a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Archduke Ferdinand Maximilian of Austria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maximilian I was an Austrian archduke, younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph I</a>, who accepted the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> offered by conservatives backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>. He arrived in 1864 to rule the Second Mexican Empire, but his liberal policies alienated his supporters while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Benito Juárez</a>'s republicans fought on. When French troops withdrew under American pressure his regime collapsed; he was captured at Querétaro and shot on 19 June 1867.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-of-mexico.jpg" alt="Maximilian I of Mexico" width="320" height="470" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original glass negative (LC-BH824-4754) made by Andrew Burgess, one of Mathew Brady's operators, on a trip to Mexico ... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maximilian_of_Mexico_bw.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
